\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Near-infrared spectroscopy (NIRS) is a rapid and non-destructive analytical
technique, but reliable calibration still depends strongly on spectral
preprocessing.  Conventional workflows treat preprocessing as an external
search over filters, scatter corrections, baseline corrections and model
hyperparameters.  This paper presents operator-adaptive calibration, a
framework that moves strict linear preprocessing alternatives inside the
calibration model while retaining fold-local handling for fitted or
sample-adaptive corrections such as SNV, MSC and ASLS.

The framework is instantiated for PLS, Ridge and FastAOM.  AOM-PLS uses
operator identities on cross-covariances to select spectral operators while
recovering original-wavelength coefficients.  AOM-Ridge uses
operator-induced kernels to adapt the regularized spectral geometry.  FastAOM
extends the same paradigm to chain exploration through low-rank screening and
sparse multi-kernel fitting.  The methods were evaluated on a heterogeneous
local NIRS benchmark with 61 regression rows in the manifest, 50--53 paired
datasets for the main AOM/FastAOM regression comparisons, and secondary
classification validation.

ASLS-AOM-compact-cv5 achieved a median RMSEP/PLS-standard ratio of 0.962 on
53 paired datasets with 37 wins and Holm-adjusted Wilcoxon $p=0.043$.
AOMRidge-Blender achieved a median RMSEP/Ridge-default ratio of 0.913 on 52
datasets with 44 wins and $p=2.8\times10^{-6}$, and remained favourable
against the cartesian Ridge-HPO baseline on the 34-dataset overlap
(ratio 0.956, 27 wins, $p=0.011$).  The best FastAOM variant,
FastAOM-sparse-mkr-supervised, reached a median RMSEP/PLS-standard ratio of
0.953 on 50 datasets with 35 wins, while the compact sparse-MKR variant gave
nearly the same paired ratio at a median fit time of 2.48 s.  A full
cartesian preprocessing-HPO baseline was usable on 36 PLS and 35 Ridge
datasets with three successful seeds; it sharply increased compute cost and
reduced the AOM-PLS margin, whereas AOM-Ridge and FastAOM retained favourable
median ratios on their overlaps.  Operator-adaptive calibration therefore
offers a practical route to faster, more auditable and still interpretable
NIRS method development.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} near-infrared spectroscopy; analytical calibration;
chemometrics; preprocessing selection; partial least squares; ridge
regression; model robustness; spectral data analysis; interpretable machine
learning.

\section{Introduction}

Near-infrared spectroscopy is widely used as a rapid, non-destructive
analytical technique for food, agriculture, plant phenotyping and process
control \citep{burns2007handbook,pasquini2018near}.  Its practical bottleneck
is often not spectral acquisition but calibration development.  A small change
in smoothing, derivative order, scatter correction or baseline correction can
change a PLS model more than changing the regression algorithm itself.  In
routine chemometric workflows, these choices are usually made through an
external preprocessing and hyperparameter search.

That workflow is familiar, but it is expensive and difficult to audit.
Preprocessing is a modelling decision: it controls which spectral directions
are visible to the regressor, how nuisance variation is suppressed, and which
coefficient profile is ultimately deployed.  Treating this decision as a
large external grid or Optuna search can be defensible in a benchmark, but it
also increases development time and selection variance, especially in
small-$n$ NIRS settings where validation folds are noisy.

We propose a different calibration paradigm.  Strict linear preprocessing
alternatives are represented as a finite operator bank
$\mathcal A=\{\A_1,\ldots,\A_m\}$ and selected inside the calibration model.
The identity operator is always included, so the standard model remains
available.  Sample-adaptive or fitted transformations such as SNV, MSC, EMSC
and ASLS are not forced into this algebra; they remain explicit fold-local
branches.  The resulting calibration object records which operator geometry
was selected and still maps original spectra to responses through
original-wavelength coefficients.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_concept.pdf}
  \caption{From external preprocessing search to operator-adaptive analytical
  calibration.  Linear preprocessing alternatives become structured model
  components; fitted or sample-adaptive corrections remain fold-local
  branches.}
  \label{fig:concept}
\end{figure}

This work makes four contributions.  First, it formalizes
operator-adaptive calibration for NIRS.  Second, it gives PLS and Ridge
instantiations that retain original-space coefficients.  Third, it introduces
FastAOM, a low-rank screening framework for exploring chains and
operator-induced kernels without fitting every candidate pipeline naively.
Fourth, it compares the resulting models with standard PLS/Ridge and with a
cartesian preprocessing-HPO protocol matching the TabPFN-paper style:
5 normalizations, 10 smoothing choices, 3 baseline choices and 4 OSC choices,
with per-combination Optuna searches.  This comparison is central because it
measures not only predictive accuracy, but the cost of obtaining that
accuracy.

\section{Related work}

External preprocessing search is the dominant practical strategy in NIRS
calibration.  Reviews of spectral preprocessing emphasize scatter correction,
baseline correction, smoothing and derivatives as necessary tools
\citep{rinnan2009review}.  Savitzky--Golay filters
\citep{savitzky1964smoothing}, Norris--Williams derivatives
\citep{norris1976influence}, SNV \citep{barnes1989standard}, MSC
\citep{geladi1985linearization}, EMSC \citep{martens1991extended} and ASLS
\citep{eilers2003perfect,zhang2010baseline} all encode meaningful
spectroscopic assumptions.  The difficulty is that large combinations of
these tools become a model-selection problem rather than a simple
preprocessing step.

PLS remains the standard linear calibration method in chemometrics
\citep{geladi1986partial,wold2001pls}, with NIPALS and SIMPLS giving
complementary computational views \citep{dejong1993simpls}.  POP-style
component-wise preprocessing extends this tradition by allowing different
preprocessing choices across latent components, but the present regression
results show that this flexibility is not automatically beneficial.  In this
benchmark, POP-PLS regression variants are exploratory rather than
competitive; the SIMPLS POP-PLS-DA classifier is useful in classification.

Ridge regression \citep{hoerl1970ridge} and kernel methods
\citep{scholkopf2002learning,hastie2009elements} provide the second foundation
for this work.  In the dual, Ridge depends on the sample Gram matrix, so
preprocessing can be expressed as an operator-induced geometry.  FastAOM uses
this view together with low-rank approximations and screened operator chains:
operator dictionaries become a structured search space, not a collection of
black-box pipelines.

\section{Methods}

\subsection{Operator-adaptive calibration}

Let $\X\in\R^{n\times p}$ be a centered spectral matrix and
$\Y\in\R^{n\times q}$ a centered response matrix.  A strict linear spectral
operator $\A_b\in\R^{p\times p}$ acts on row spectra as
\begin{equation}
  \X_b = \X\A_b\transpose .
\end{equation}
An operator-adaptive calibration model evaluates the finite bank
$\mathcal A$ inside the calibration procedure and stores the selected
operator information with the fitted model.  The bank includes identity,
Savitzky--Golay smoothers and derivatives, polynomial detrending projections
and finite differences.  Table~\ref{tab:operators} separates strict operators
from branch preprocessors.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Compact operator bank and branch preprocessing distinction.}
  \label{tab:operators}
  \small
  \input{tables/table_operator_bank.tex}
\end{table}

The strict distinction is essential.  SNV divides by sample-specific
statistics; MSC and EMSC estimate correction parameters relative to a
reference; ASLS estimates a baseline through an iterative asymmetric
criterion.  These are valid preprocessing branches, but they are not fixed
matrices that can be inserted into the operator algebra outside a fold.

\subsection{AOM-PLS}

For centered $\X$ and $\Y$, a PLS model with weights $\mathbf W$, loadings
$\mathbf P$ and response loadings $\mathbf Q$ has coefficient matrix
\begin{equation}
  \B = \mathbf W(\mathbf P\transpose \mathbf W)^+ \mathbf Q\transpose ,
\end{equation}
where $+$ denotes the Moore--Penrose inverse.  AOM-PLS evaluates operator
candidates through their action on the cross-covariance:
\begin{equation}
  (\X\A_b\transpose)\transpose\Y = \A_b\X\transpose\Y .
  \label{eq:cov_identity}
\end{equation}

