\end{equation}
This identity supports covariance-space SIMPLS and adjoint NIPALS
implementations without materializing every transformed spectrum matrix.

If the selected transformed-space direction for component $a$ is $r_a$, its
original-space direction is
\begin{equation}
  z_a = \A_b\transpose r_a .
\end{equation}
Collecting the selected directions in $\Z=[z_1,\ldots,z_k]$ gives scores
$\T=\X\Z$ and the final coefficient matrix
\begin{equation}
  \B = \Z(\mathbf P\transpose \Z)^+ \mathbf Q\transpose .
  \label{eq:aompls_coef}
\end{equation}
Thus internal operator selection still yields one linear model on the original
wavelength grid.

The main manuscript reports the three AOM-PLS variants that remained
performant after multi-seed aggregation: \texttt{AOM-compact-cv5},
\texttt{ASLS-AOM-compact-cv5}, and
\texttt{AOM-default-nipals-adjoint}.  The compact bank contains nine
operators.  The supplement gives the exploration path that led to this bank;
the summary statistic is that the three most frequent strict operators
(\texttt{sg\_smooth\_w21\_p3}, \texttt{sg\_d1\_w21\_p3} and
\texttt{sg\_d1\_w11\_p2}) account for 54.7\% of compact-bank component
selections.

\subsection{AOM-Ridge}

For centered spectra $\X_c$ and response $\Y_c$, a single operator gives the
dual Ridge kernel
\begin{equation}
  \K_b = \X_c\A_b\transpose\A_b\X_c\transpose .
  \label{eq:ridge_kernel}
\end{equation}
For regularization parameter $\alpha>0$,
\begin{equation}
  \mathbf C = (\K_b+\alpha\mathbf I_n)^{-1}\Y_c ,
  \qquad
  \bm\beta_b = \A_b\transpose\A_b\X_c\transpose\mathbf C .
  \label{eq:ridge_beta}
\end{equation}
Weighted superblocks combine several operator geometries:
\begin{equation}
  \K = \sum_{b=1}^{m}s_b^2\X_c\A_b\transpose\A_b\X_c\transpose,
  \qquad
  \bm\beta = \left(\sum_{b=1}^{m}s_b^2\A_b\transpose\A_b\right)
  \X_c\transpose(\K+\alpha\mathbf I_n)^{-1}\Y_c .
\end{equation}

The main AOM-Ridge variants are Blender, AutoSelect,
\texttt{global-compact-none} and \texttt{Local-knn50}.  Blender averages
several strong operator geometries through nested validation.  AutoSelect
chooses among the same candidates.  The global compact variant uses one
operator geometry on the complete calibration set, while Local-knn50 restricts
the geometry to local neighbourhoods.  The full exploration of selector
stability and the top5\_fast partial run is reported in the supplement.

