  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_math.pdf}
  \caption{AOM mathematical structure.  AOM-PLS exploits an operator identity
  on cross-covariances, while AOM-Ridge builds operator-specific sample
  kernels.  Both recover original-space coefficients.}
  \label{fig:math}
\end{figure}

\subsection{FastAOM}

FastAOM explores a larger structured space of preprocessing chains without
fitting one complete model per chain.  The end-to-end
\texttt{FastAOMPLSRidge} orchestrator builds fold-local bases, enumerates
grammar-pruned operator chains, computes low-rank summaries, screens
candidates, applies diversity-aware top-$k$ caps, and fits one of four model
families.

Let $B_j(\X)$ be a fold-local base representation such as raw spectra, SNV,
MSC, EMSC or ASLS-transformed spectra.  For a chain $s$ and response vector
$y$, the screening score used in FastAOM is
\begin{equation}
  \mathrm{score}(j,s)
  =
  \frac{\left\|A_s B_j(\X_c)\transpose y_c\right\|_2^2}
       {\left\|B_j(\X_c)A_s\transpose\right\|_F^2
        \left\|y_c\right\|_2^2}.
  \label{eq:fastaom_screen}
\end{equation}
If $B_j(\X_c)\approx U_j\Sigma_jV_j\transpose$ is a truncated SVD and
$F_{j,s}=A_sV_j\Sigma_j$, then the screened operator kernel is approximated as
\begin{equation}
  \K_{j,s}
  =
  B_j(\X_c)A_s\transpose A_s B_j(\X_c)\transpose
  \approx U_j C_{j,s}U_j\transpose,
  \qquad
  C_{j,s}=F_{j,s}\transpose F_{j,s}.
  \label{eq:fastaom_kernel}
\end{equation}
Kernel-vector products are therefore reduced to low-rank operations during
screening.

The four FastAOM model families are SingleChainPLSRidge, HardAOMChainPLSRidge,
SoftAOMChainPLSRidge and SparseMultiKernelRidge.  The main results emphasize
the two performant model classes: SparseMultiKernelRidge, which greedily
selects chains in a sparse non-negative kernel mixture, and
SingleChainPLSRidge, which fits PLS-Ridge on the top screened chain.  The hard
and soft chain-routing strategies are part of the exploration reported in the
supplement.

\section{Datasets and protocol}
\label{sec:datasets}

The cohort manifest contains 61 included local regression rows and 16 included
classification rows.  The main AOM-PLS paired comparison uses 53 non-QUARTZ
datasets with paired multi-seed AOM-PLS and PLS-standard rows.  FastAOM
headline variants use 50--52 successful full-cohort datasets after filtering
to variants with at least 50 completed rows.  The cartesian linear-HPO
baselines use the complete successful subset: 36 PLS-HPO datasets and 35
Ridge-HPO datasets with three successful seeds.  The LUCAS\_SOC subset
exceeded the available compute budget in that cartesian campaign, and two
FUSARIUM response rows failed estimator checks because the response contained
NaN values.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Benchmark diversity and denominator rules used in this work.}
  \label{tab:benchmark_diversity}
  \small
  \input{tables/table_benchmark_diversity.tex}
\end{table}

All preprocessing and hyperparameter choices are made inside the calibration
set.  External test sets are preserved from source data when available; when
not available, deterministic SPXY or stratified splits are used
\citep{kennard1969computer,galvao2005method}.  RMSEP is the primary
regression metric.  Because response scales differ across traits, aggregate
comparisons use paired RMSEP ratios.  Classification uses balanced accuracy.

\section{Results}

\subsection{AOM-PLS regression}

The strongest deployable AOM-PLS result is ASLS branch preprocessing followed
by compact AOM-PLS with 5-fold internal selection.  Against the benchmark
PLS-standard reference, it reaches a median RMSEP ratio of 0.962 on 53 paired
datasets, with 37 wins and Holm-adjusted Wilcoxon $p=0.043$
(Table~\ref{tab:paired_stats}).  The strict compact AOM-PLS variant also
improves the median ratio, 0.982 with 36 wins and $p=0.034$.  The
default-bank NIPALS-adjoint variant is included as a performant reference but
is neutral at the aggregate level, with median ratio 0.999.

The complete preprocessing-HPO comparison changes the interpretation.  On the
32-dataset overlap with successful PLS-HPO rows, ASLS-AOM is essentially tied
with PLS-HPO (median ratio 1.002, 15 wins), while strict compact AOM-PLS is
slightly favourable at the median (0.990, 19 wins).  This shows that a full
cartesian HPO campaign can recover part of the preprocessing advantage, but it
does so at a much larger compute cost and on a reduced completed subset.

