\subsection{AOM-Ridge regression}

AOM-Ridge gives the clearest gain against the linear Ridge baselines.  Blender
achieves a median RMSEP/Ridge-default ratio of 0.913 on 52 datasets, with
44 wins and $p=2.8\times10^{-6}$.  Against the stronger cartesian Ridge-HPO
baseline, Blender remains favourable on the 34-dataset overlap: median ratio
0.956, 27 wins and $p=0.011$.  AutoSelect has the same median ratio against
Ridge-HPO (0.956) but fewer wins (24/34), while the global compact variant is
nearer parity (0.978, 21/34).  Local-knn50 is computationally efficient, but
the current overlap with Ridge-HPO is unfavourable (ratio 1.173, 6/25 wins);
it is therefore treated as an efficiency-oriented exploration rather than the
headline Ridge selector.

The Ridge result supports the operator-geometry view: adapting the spectral
Gram matrix is at least as important as adapting the cross-covariance in PLS,
and the Blender selector gives a robust middle ground between single-operator
selection and broad superblock mixtures.

\subsection{FastAOM regression}

Filtering FastAOM variants to at least 50 successful datasets gives three
performant entries.  FastAOM-sparse-mkr-supervised has the best FastAOM
summary statistic in the full60 seed0 table, with median relative RMSEP 1.009,
50 datasets, 10 headline wins and median fit time 87.77 s.  The compact
SparseMultiKernelRidge variant is almost as accurate in the summary
(median relative RMSEP 1.022, 50 datasets) and much faster (2.48 s).  The
SingleChainPLSRidge compact variant reaches median relative RMSEP 1.052 on 52
datasets at 1.86 s.

Paired against PLS-standard, FastAOM-sparse-mkr-supervised reaches median
ratio 0.953 with 35 wins on 50 datasets.  The compact sparse-MKR variant has
the same median paired ratio, also with 35 wins, indicating that most of the
benefit comes from the screened multi-kernel formulation rather than the
supervised branch alone.  Against PLS-HPO on the 35-dataset overlap,
FastAOM-sparse-mkr-supervised remains favourable at the median (0.968, 19
wins), but the Holm-adjusted paired test is not significant.  Against the
ASLS-AOM-compact-cv5 reference in the same wide table, FastAOM-sparse-mkr
supervised is slightly behind at the median (1.025, 23/50 wins).  The common
six-method Friedman analysis confirms that these differences are structured
($N=50$, $\chi^2=29.920$, $p=1.53\times10^{-5}$): mean ranks are 2.74 for
ASLS-AOM, 3.08 for FastAOM-sparse-mkr-supervised, 3.18 for
FastAOM-sparse-mkr-compact, 3.44 for AOM-compact, 4.20 for
FastAOM-single-chain, and 4.36 for PLS-standard.

