\section{Notation and Linear-Operator Scope}

Let $\X\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times p}$ be a spectral matrix and
$\Y\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times q}$ a response matrix.  A strict linear spectral
operator $\A_b\in\mathbb{R}^{p\times p}$ acts on row spectra as
\begin{equation}
  \X_b=\X\A_b\transpose .
\end{equation}
The operator bank contains the identity operator, so ordinary PLS or Ridge is
always included in the candidate set.  Strict operators in this work include
Savitzky--Golay smoothers, Savitzky--Golay derivatives, finite differences
and polynomial detrending projections.

The strict-linear scope does not include sample-adaptive or fitted
transformations.  SNV uses sample-specific mean and scale statistics, MSC and
EMSC estimate correction parameters relative to a reference, and ASLS solves
an asymmetric baseline problem.  These transformations are used as
fold-local branches.  This separation prevents leakage and keeps the operator
algebra mathematically valid.

\section{Full Mathematical Derivations}

\subsection{AOM-PLS covariance identity}

For centered $\X$ and $\Y$, standard PLS can be written with weights
$\mathbf W$, loadings $\mathbf P$ and response loadings $\mathbf Q$:
\begin{equation}
  \B=\mathbf W(\mathbf P\transpose\mathbf W)^+\mathbf Q\transpose .
\end{equation}
For a fixed linear operator $\A_b$, the transformed cross-covariance is
\begin{equation}
  (\X\A_b\transpose)\transpose\Y
  =
  \A_b\X\transpose\Y .
  \label{eq:sup_cov_identity}
\end{equation}
Equation~\ref{eq:sup_cov_identity} permits SIMPLS-style covariance evaluation
and NIPALS-style adjoint evaluation without materializing every transformed
matrix.  If the transformed-space direction is $r_a$, its original-space
direction is $z_a=\A_b\transpose r_a$.  With
$\Z=[z_1,\ldots,z_k]$ and scores $\T=\X\Z$, the original-space coefficient
matrix is
\begin{equation}
  \B=\Z(\mathbf P\transpose\Z)^+\mathbf Q\transpose .
\end{equation}
The fitted AOM-PLS model is therefore one linear calibration on the original
wavelength grid.

\subsection{AOM-Ridge dual geometry}

For centered $\X_c$ and $\Y_c$, Ridge regression can be solved in the dual.
A single operator induces
\begin{align}
  \mathbf Z_b &= \X_c\A_b\transpose,\\
  \K_b &= \mathbf Z_b\mathbf Z_b\transpose
       = \X_c\A_b\transpose\A_b\X_c\transpose,\\
  \mathbf C_b &= (\K_b+\alpha\mathbf I_n)^{-1}\Y_c,
\end{align}
and the recovered original-space coefficient matrix is
\begin{equation}
  \bm\beta_b=\A_b\transpose\A_b\X_c\transpose\mathbf C_b .
\end{equation}
For a weighted operator mixture,
\begin{equation}
  \K=\sum_{b=1}^{m}s_b^2\X_c\A_b\transpose\A_b\X_c\transpose,
  \qquad
  \mathbf M=\sum_{b=1}^{m}s_b^2\A_b\transpose\A_b,
\end{equation}
so
\begin{equation}
  \bm\beta=\mathbf M\X_c\transpose(\K+\alpha\mathbf I_n)^{-1}\Y_c.
\end{equation}
This is the dual counterpart of AOM-PLS: PLS adapts supervised
cross-covariance directions, whereas Ridge adapts the regularized sample
geometry.

\subsection{FastAOM chain grammar and simplification}

FastAOM defines a chain as an ordered composition of primitive strict
operators.  The grammar assigns roles to operators: baseline/projection,
smoothing, derivative and projection-mask operations.  A depth cap limits the
number of composed operators.  The compact runs use depth 3 for most variants,
while the depth-4 hard-chain diagnostic completed on one dataset.  Grammar
pruning removes invalid or redundant role sequences before screening.  The
simplifier keeps only algebraically safe reductions inherited from the parent
AOM implementation, such as identity removal and consecutive detrend collapse;
it does not collapse different smoothers or remove valid derivative-boundary
chains.

For each fold-local base $B_j(\X)$ and chain $s$, FastAOM computes the
screening score
\begin{equation}
  \mathrm{score}(j,s)
  =
  \frac{\left\|A_s B_j(\X_c)\transpose y_c\right\|_2^2}
       {\left\|B_j(\X_c)A_s\transpose\right\|_F^2
        \left\|y_c\right\|_2^2}.
\end{equation}
The denominator is estimated from a truncated SVD
$B_j(\X_c)\approx U_j\Sigma_jV_j\transpose$.  With
$F_{j,s}=A_sV_j\Sigma_j$,
\begin{equation}
  \K_{j,s}\approx U_jC_{j,s}U_j\transpose,
  \qquad
  C_{j,s}=F_{j,s}\transpose F_{j,s}.
\end{equation}
This gives low-rank kernel-vector products during screening and permits
diversity-aware top-$k$ filtering by base and operator family.

\subsection{FastAOM model variants}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{FastAOM model families.  The main paper emphasizes the sparse
multi-kernel and single-chain families; hard and soft chain routing are
reported as exploration.}
\small
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{p{0.25\linewidth}p{0.25\linewidth}X}
\toprule
Class & Routing strategy & Role in this work \\
\midrule
\code{SingleChainPLSRidge} & Top screened chain followed by PLS-Ridge &
Fast baseline equivalent to selecting one strong chain and fitting a
coefficient-bearing calibration.\\
\code{HardAOMChainPLSRidge} & One greedy chain per latent component &
Faithful chain-level AOM exploration; useful diagnostically but not promoted
