\section{Operator Bank Exploration}

The compact bank contains nine operators:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\mathcal A_{\mathrm{compact}}=\{&
\mathrm{identity},
\mathrm{SG}_{11,2}^{(0)},\mathrm{SG}_{21,3}^{(0)},
\mathrm{SG}_{11,2}^{(1)},\mathrm{SG}_{21,3}^{(1)},\\
&\mathrm{SG}_{11,2}^{(2)},
\mathrm{detrend}_{1},\mathrm{detrend}_{2},
\mathrm{FD}_{1}\}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
It is deliberately small.  Larger banks increase the number of candidates and
therefore increase the risk of selecting validation noise.  With $B$
operators and $K_{\max}$ component prefixes, a global selector considers
approximately $BK_{\max}$ operator/component candidates before fold
replication.  At $K_{\max}=15$, the compact bank has 135 candidates, whereas
the 100-operator default bank has 1500.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Compact-bank operator frequencies from the refreshed selector
diagnostics.}
\label{tab:sup_selector}
\small
\input{tables/table_selector_diagnostics.tex}
\end{table}

The operator-frequency table shows that the compact bank does not collapse to
one preprocessing choice.  The wider Savitzky--Golay smoother
\code{sg\_smooth\_w21\_p3} is the most frequent selected operator
(23.4\% of selected components).  First-derivative operators occupy the next
two positions, and finite difference plus detrending operators form the
middle of the distribution.  Identity remains selected on 8.7\% of selected
components, which confirms that the bank can fall back to raw centered
spectra when preprocessing is not beneficial.

\begin{figure}[h]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_operator_heatmap.pdf}
  \caption{Per-dataset compact-bank operator heatmap.  Rows are ordered by
  manifest domain group and dataset name; cells count selected components
  across the compact AOM-PLS variants and seeds.}
  \label{fig:sup_operator_heatmap}
\end{figure}

The heatmap highlights three patterns.  First, derivative operators dominate
many trait groups where local spectral contrast is useful.  Second, detrending
and finite-difference operators appear as dataset-specific alternatives rather
than global defaults.  Third, datasets that do not benefit from the compact
bank are not concentrated in one family; they occur when the default wider
bank or a branch correction better matches the validation split.  Quantitatively,
compact AOM-PLS beats the production default on 34 of 53 paired datasets, and
strict compact AOM-PLS beats the ASLS branch on 23 of 53 datasets.  The ASLS
branch is therefore useful but not universally dominant.

