-This work makes four contributions.  First, it formalizes
-operator-adaptive calibration for NIRS.  Second, it gives PLS and Ridge
-instantiations that retain original-space coefficients.  Third, it introduces
-FastAOM, a low-rank screening framework for exploring chains and
-operator-induced kernels without fitting every candidate pipeline naively.
-Fourth, it compares the resulting models with standard PLS/Ridge and with a
-cartesian preprocessing-HPO protocol matching the TabPFN-paper style:
-5 normalizations, 10 smoothing choices, 3 baseline choices and 4 OSC choices,
-with per-combination Optuna searches.  This comparison is central because it
-measures not only predictive accuracy, but the cost of obtaining that
-accuracy.
-
 \section{Related work}
 
-External preprocessing search is the dominant practical strategy in NIRS
-calibration.  Reviews of spectral preprocessing emphasize scatter correction,
-baseline correction, smoothing and derivatives as necessary tools
-\citep{rinnan2009review}.  Savitzky--Golay filters
+Spectral preprocessing is a central part of chemometrics.  Common tools
+include Savitzky--Golay smoothing and derivatives
 \citep{savitzky1964smoothing}, Norris--Williams derivatives
 \citep{norris1976influence}, SNV \citep{barnes1989standard}, MSC
-\citep{geladi1985linearization}, EMSC \citep{martens1991extended} and ASLS
-\citep{eilers2003perfect,zhang2010baseline} all encode meaningful
-spectroscopic assumptions.  The difficulty is that large combinations of
-these tools become a model-selection problem rather than a simple
-preprocessing step.
-
-PLS remains the standard linear calibration method in chemometrics
-\citep{geladi1986partial,wold2001pls}, with NIPALS and SIMPLS giving
-complementary computational views \citep{dejong1993simpls}.  POP-style
-component-wise preprocessing extends this tradition by allowing different
-preprocessing choices across latent components, but the present regression
-results show that this flexibility is not automatically beneficial.  In this
-benchmark, POP-PLS regression variants are exploratory rather than
-competitive; the SIMPLS POP-PLS-DA classifier is useful in classification.
+\citep{geladi1985linearization}, EMSC \citep{martens1991extended} and
+penalized baseline smoothers \citep{eilers2003perfect,zhang2010baseline}.
+Reviews emphasize that these transformations remove different nuisance
+structures and should be chosen with the analytical setting in mind
+\citep{rinnan2009review}.
 
-Ridge regression \citep{hoerl1970ridge} and kernel methods
-\citep{scholkopf2002learning,hastie2009elements} provide the second foundation
-for this work.  In the dual, Ridge depends on the sample Gram matrix, so
-preprocessing can be expressed as an operator-induced geometry.  FastAOM uses
-this view together with low-rank approximations and screened operator chains:
-operator dictionaries become a structured search space, not a collection of
-black-box pipelines.
+PLS remains the reference linear method for many NIRS calibration problems
+\citep{geladi1986partial,wold2001pls}.  NIPALS and SIMPLS offer two useful
+views: one iterative and score-based, the other covariance-based
+\citep{dejong1993simpls}.  Ridge regression provides a complementary
+regularized linear model \citep{hoerl1970ridge}; in dual form it depends on
+the sample Gram matrix, which makes preprocessing a change of kernel geometry
+\citep{scholkopf2002learning,hastie2009elements}.
 
-\section{Methods}
+This work combines these two lines.  It keeps the chemometric interpretability
+of linear calibration, but treats strict preprocessing alternatives as
+operators that can be selected inside the model rather than as independent
+outer-loop pipelines.
 
