 \section{Introduction}
 
-Near-infrared spectroscopy is widely used as a rapid, non-destructive
-analytical technique for food, agriculture, plant phenotyping and process
-control \citep{burns2007handbook,pasquini2018near}.  Its practical bottleneck
-is often not spectral acquisition but calibration development.  A small change
-in smoothing, derivative order, scatter correction or baseline correction can
-change a PLS model more than changing the regression algorithm itself.  In
-routine chemometric workflows, these choices are usually made through an
-external preprocessing and hyperparameter search.
+Near-infrared spectroscopy (NIRS) is used because it is rapid,
+non-destructive and compatible with routine analytical deployment
+\citep{burns2007handbook,pasquini2018near}.  The calibration step is still
+fragile.  A model that works on raw spectra may fail after a different split,
+instrument or sample family, while the same regressor with a derivative,
+smoother or baseline correction can become usable.  In practice, much of the
+model-building effort is therefore spent screening preprocessing choices.
+
+The usual response is external search.  A calibration workflow enumerates
+normalizations, smoothers, derivative orders, baseline corrections and model
+hyperparameters, then chooses the best validated pipeline.  That workflow is
+straightforward and often effective, but it is slow.  It also hides a
+scientific question inside a large engineering loop: which spectral linear
+geometry does the calibration model need?
 
-That workflow is familiar, but it is expensive and difficult to audit.
-Preprocessing is a modelling decision: it controls which spectral directions
-are visible to the regressor, how nuisance variation is suppressed, and which
-coefficient profile is ultimately deployed.  Treating this decision as a
-large external grid or Optuna search can be defensible in a benchmark, but it
-also increases development time and selection variance, especially in
-small-$n$ NIRS settings where validation folds are noisy.
+This paper focuses on the part of preprocessing that has a simple algebraic
+answer.  If a preprocessing step is a fixed linear operator on the wavelength
+axis, it can be represented by a matrix $\A$ and moved inside PLS or Ridge
+calibration.  The model can then screen a bank of such operators in one fit
+rather than refitting every transformed data matrix as an external pipeline.
+This does not claim that every preprocessing step is linear.  SNV, MSC, EMSC
+and ASLS are fitted or sample-adaptive transformations; they remain
+fold-local branches in the experimental protocol.
 
-We propose a different calibration paradigm.  Strict linear preprocessing
-alternatives are represented as a finite operator bank
-$\mathcal A=\{\A_1,\ldots,\A_m\}$ and selected inside the calibration model.
-The identity operator is always included, so the standard model remains
-available.  Sample-adaptive or fitted transformations such as SNV, MSC, EMSC
-and ASLS are not forced into this algebra; they remain explicit fold-local
-branches.  The resulting calibration object records which operator geometry
-was selected and still maps original spectra to responses through
-original-wavelength coefficients.
+The contribution is deliberately narrow.  We derive the PLS covariance
+identity and the Ridge operator-kernel identity, instantiate them in AOM-PLS
+and AOM-Ridge, and test the resulting models against default PLS/Ridge and
+full cartesian preprocessing-HPO baselines.  The question is not whether a
+new nonlinear learner can win a benchmark.  The question is whether the
+preprocessing search already used with linear chemometric models can be made
+faster, simpler and easier to audit.
 
