-\subsection{AOM-Ridge regression}
-
-AOM-Ridge gives the clearest gain against the linear Ridge baselines.  Blender
-achieves a median RMSEP/Ridge-default ratio of 0.913 on 52 datasets, with
-44 wins and $p=2.8\times10^{-6}$.  Against the stronger cartesian Ridge-HPO
-baseline, Blender remains favourable on the 34-dataset overlap: median ratio
-0.956, 27 wins and $p=0.011$.  AutoSelect has the same median ratio against
-Ridge-HPO (0.956) but fewer wins (24/34), while the global compact variant is
-nearer parity (0.978, 21/34).  Local-knn50 is computationally efficient, but
-the current overlap with Ridge-HPO is unfavourable (ratio 1.173, 6/25 wins);
-it is therefore treated as an efficiency-oriented exploration rather than the
-headline Ridge selector.
-
-The Ridge result supports the operator-geometry view: adapting the spectral
-Gram matrix is at least as important as adapting the cross-covariance in PLS,
-and the Blender selector gives a robust middle ground between single-operator
-selection and broad superblock mixtures.
