+\subsection{Time budget}
 
-The cartesian linear-HPO baselines make the cost of external preprocessing
-optimization explicit.  PLS-HPO used 600 preprocessing combinations with five
-trials each and produced 36 complete three-seed datasets; the observed total
-time across successful rows is 162.4 h, with median per-run total time
-984.90 s.  Ridge-HPO used ten trials per combination and produced 35 complete
-datasets; the observed total is 184.1 h, with median per-run total time
-2024.55 s.  By contrast, ASLS-AOM-compact-cv5 has a median fit time of 1.89 s
-in the multi-seed run, FastAOM-sparse-mkr-compact 2.48 s, and
-FastAOM-single-chain-compact 1.86 s.  AOMRidge-Blender is heavier
-(median 960.08 s) but remains competitive with Ridge-HPO while retaining a
-single auditable operator-kernel model.
+The practical advantage is compute.  PLS-HPO has a median total time of
+984.90 s per completed run and an observed successful-row total of 162.4 h.
+The selected AOM-PLS branch has a median total time of 1.94 s and an observed
+total of 0.2 h.  On median total time, this is a factor of about 508.  In
+candidate evaluations, the contrast is 3000 trial fits per PLS-HPO
+dataset/seed versus 45 compact-bank CV evaluations for AOM-PLS.
+
+Ridge is different because the selected Blender selector is heavier.  It
+still improves over Ridge-HPO on the completed overlap, and its median total
+time is 960.71 s compared with 2024.55 s for Ridge-HPO.  The result is not
+that every AOM selector is instant; it is that strict-linear preprocessing
+can be represented as a model geometry rather than as a blind external
+pipeline loop.
 
