 \begin{abstract}
-Near-infrared spectroscopy (NIRS) is a rapid and non-destructive analytical
-technique, but reliable calibration still depends strongly on spectral
-preprocessing.  Conventional workflows treat preprocessing as an external
-search over filters, scatter corrections, baseline corrections and model
-hyperparameters.  This paper presents operator-adaptive calibration, a
-framework that moves strict linear preprocessing alternatives inside the
-calibration model while retaining fold-local handling for fitted or
-sample-adaptive corrections such as SNV, MSC and ASLS.
+Preprocessing screening is often the most expensive part of a near-infrared
+spectroscopy calibration workflow.  It works because smoothing, derivatives,
+detrending and related filters change the spectral directions seen by PLS or
+Ridge regression, but a full external search repeatedly refits nearly the same
+linear model.  This paper studies the case where that search can be collapsed
+into one calibration step.
 
-The framework is instantiated for PLS, Ridge and FastAOM.  AOM-PLS uses
-operator identities on cross-covariances to select spectral operators while
-recovering original-wavelength coefficients.  AOM-Ridge uses
-operator-induced kernels to adapt the regularized spectral geometry.  FastAOM
-extends the same paradigm to chain exploration through low-rank screening and
-sparse multi-kernel fitting.  The methods were evaluated on a heterogeneous
-local NIRS benchmark with 61 regression rows in the manifest, 50--53 paired
-datasets for the main AOM/FastAOM regression comparisons, and secondary
-classification validation.
+For strict linear preprocessing operators, the transformed PLS
+cross-covariance satisfies
+$(\X\A^\transpose)^\transpose\Y=\A\X^\transpose\Y$, and Ridge regression
+depends on the operator-induced kernel
+$\X\A^\transpose\A\X^\transpose$.  These identities allow a finite operator
+bank to be screened inside the model while retaining original-wavelength
+coefficients.  Sample-adaptive or fitted corrections such as SNV, MSC, EMSC
+and ASLS remain fold-local branches, not absorbed into the algebra.
 
-ASLS-AOM-compact-cv5 achieved a median RMSEP/PLS-standard ratio of 0.962 on
-53 paired datasets with 37 wins and Holm-adjusted Wilcoxon $p=0.043$.
-AOMRidge-Blender achieved a median RMSEP/Ridge-default ratio of 0.913 on 52
-datasets with 44 wins and $p=2.8\times10^{-6}$, and remained favourable
-against the cartesian Ridge-HPO baseline on the 34-dataset overlap
-(ratio 0.956, 27 wins, $p=0.011$).  The best FastAOM variant,
-FastAOM-sparse-mkr-supervised, reached a median RMSEP/PLS-standard ratio of
-0.953 on 50 datasets with 35 wins, while the compact sparse-MKR variant gave
-nearly the same paired ratio at a median fit time of 2.48 s.  A full
-cartesian preprocessing-HPO baseline was usable on 36 PLS and 35 Ridge
-datasets with three successful seeds; it sharply increased compute cost and
-reduced the AOM-PLS margin, whereas AOM-Ridge and FastAOM retained favourable
-median ratios on their overlaps.  Operator-adaptive calibration therefore
-offers a practical route to faster, more auditable and still interpretable
-NIRS method development.
+The study uses the full available AOM benchmark cohort: 61 regression rows in
+the manifest, 53 AOM-PLS paired datasets for variant screening, 52 paired
+AOM-Ridge datasets against Ridge, 36 completed PLS-HPO datasets, 35 completed
+Ridge-HPO datasets, and 16 classification rows.  The selected PLS variant,
+\texttt{ASLS-AOM-compact-cv5}, was comparable to the completed full
+preprocessing-HPO baseline (median RMSEP ratio 1.002 on $N=32$, 15/32 wins)
+and improved over default PLS on its broad overlap (0.985 on $N=52$, 33/52
+wins).  The selected Ridge variant, \texttt{AOMRidge-Blender-headline-spxy3},
+improved over Ridge-HPO (0.956 on $N=34$, 27/34 wins) and over default Ridge
+(0.913 on $N=52$, 44/52 wins).  The selected classifier,
+\texttt{AOM-PLS-DA-global-simpls-covariance}, improved balanced accuracy by
+0.159 on $N=13$ datasets with 12/13 wins.  The runtime gap is the practical
+result: PLS-HPO took a median total time of 984.90 s per completed run,
+whereas the selected AOM-PLS branch took 1.94 s.  Linear operator-adaptive
+calibration therefore gives comparable accuracy to exhaustive preprocessing
+screening on the completed subset, with orders-of-magnitude less fitting
+time for PLS.
 \end{abstract}
 
