 \section{Discussion}
 
-The benchmark supports the central premise that preprocessing can be moved
-from external pipeline optimization into the calibration model.  AOM-PLS
-improves over the benchmark PLS-standard reference while keeping a compact,
-interpretable operator bank.  The full PLS-HPO subset shows that exhaustive
-external search can recover some of that advantage, but the cost is high and
-the completed denominator is smaller.  This is the practical tradeoff:
-external HPO can be powerful, but it produces a selected pipeline after many
-fold-level fits; AOM produces a single calibration object with logged operator
-choices.
+The benchmark supports a simple engineering conclusion: when preprocessing
+alternatives are strict linear operators, screening them outside the model is
+often unnecessary.  AOM-PLS folds the operator choice into the PLS
+cross-covariance calculation, and AOM-Ridge folds it into the Ridge kernel.
+The resulting calibration remains linear and auditable.
 
-AOM-Ridge shows the strongest statistical signal in this draft.  Its success
-indicates that NIRS preprocessing is not only about supervised
-cross-covariance, but also about the spectral geometry used by shrinkage
-methods.  Blender is the best current Ridge selector because it combines
-strong geometries without relying on a single winner from a noisy validation
-surface.
+The strongest use case is the one common in NIRS: small to moderate
+calibration sets, noisy validation folds and many plausible preprocessing
+choices.  In that regime, an exhaustive external search spends most of its
+time refitting variations of the same linear model.  Compact AOM-PLS reduces
+that cost by orders of magnitude while remaining close to the completed
+PLS-HPO baseline, and AOM-Ridge improves over both default Ridge and
+Ridge-HPO on the paired completed subset.
 
-FastAOM adds a different capability: cheap exploration of a much larger chain
-space.  Sparse multi-kernel FastAOM improves over PLS-standard and ranks close
-to ASLS-AOM, while compact sparse-MKR shows that the chain-screening idea can
-be practical at seconds-scale fitting.  The supervised sparse-MKR variant is
-more accurate in the summary but substantially slower.  This creates a useful
-optimization axis for practice: use compact SparseMKR for rapid exploration
-and supervised SparseMKR when additional screening cost is acceptable.
+The scope is also clear.  AOM algebra does not absorb sample-adaptive
+transforms.  SNV, MSC, EMSC and ASLS must be fitted inside folds.  This is why
+the selected PLS result is described as an ASLS branch followed by strict
+linear AOM, not as ASLS being part of the operator identity.  When such
+branches dominate a dataset, they should remain explicit and audited.
 
-The failure modes are also informative.  POP-PLS regression is not
-competitive at the default settings used here: the two regression POP
-references have median relative RMSEP 1.475 and 4.753 in the FastAOM summary.
-They are therefore not promoted as regression methods.  Hard-chain and
-soft-chain FastAOM variants are valuable exploration tools, but the main
-results favour sparse MKR and single-chain compact models.  Local AOM-Ridge is
-fast but not competitive with Ridge-HPO on the current overlap.
-
-The LUCAS\_SOC gap should be read as a denominator statement, not as a
-biological conclusion.  The cartesian-HPO campaign did not complete there
-within the available compute budget, while AOM and FastAOM benchmarks cover a
-broader set of rows.  All HPO comparisons in this paper are therefore stated
-on the explicit successful subsets.
+The HPO comparisons should be read with their denominators.  Cartesian-HPO
+completed on 36 PLS and 35 Ridge datasets, not on the full regression
+manifest.  AOM comparisons cover broader paired denominators, but the
+head-to-head statements against HPO are only made on the completed overlaps.
+This avoids extrapolating from unfinished compute campaigns.
 
 \section{Reproducibility}
 
-The software artifacts are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:software}.
-Code and benchmark workspaces supporting this study will be made
