-\subsection{AOM-PLS regression}
+\subsection{PLS regression}
 
-The strongest deployable AOM-PLS result is ASLS branch preprocessing followed
-by compact AOM-PLS with 5-fold internal selection.  Against the benchmark
-PLS-standard reference, it reaches a median RMSEP ratio of 0.962 on 53 paired
-datasets, with 37 wins and Holm-adjusted Wilcoxon $p=0.043$
-(Table~\ref{tab:paired_stats}).  The strict compact AOM-PLS variant also
-improves the median ratio, 0.982 with 36 wins and $p=0.034$.  The
-default-bank NIPALS-adjoint variant is included as a performant reference but
-is neutral at the aggregate level, with median ratio 0.999.
+The selected AOM-PLS regression variant is
+\texttt{ASLS-AOM-compact-cv5}.  It was selected from the largest AOM-PLS
+screening denominator and then compared in the main text only with the
+pre-registered linear references.  Against PLS-default, it reaches a median
+RMSEP ratio of 0.985 on $N=52$ datasets with 33/52 wins.  Against the
+completed PLS-HPO subset, it is essentially tied: median ratio 1.002 on
+$N=32$, 15/32 wins.
+
+This is the intended interpretation.  Full external preprocessing-HPO can
+recover the benefit of careful preprocessing, but the gain is obtained by
+repeatedly fitting the linear calibration under a much larger search budget.
+AOM-PLS makes the strict-linear part of the same decision inside one
+coefficient-bearing calibration object.
 
-The complete preprocessing-HPO comparison changes the interpretation.  On the
-32-dataset overlap with successful PLS-HPO rows, ASLS-AOM is essentially tied
-with PLS-HPO (median ratio 1.002, 15 wins), while strict compact AOM-PLS is
-slightly favourable at the median (0.990, 19 wins).  This shows that a full
-cartesian HPO campaign can recover part of the preprocessing advantage, but it
-does so at a much larger compute cost and on a reduced completed subset.
+\subsection{Ridge regression}
+
+The selected Ridge variant is
+\texttt{AOMRidge-Blender-headline-spxy3}.  It improves strongly over default
+Ridge: median RMSEP ratio 0.913 on $N=52$ datasets with 44/52 wins and
+Holm-adjusted $p=2.8\times10^{-6}$.  It also improves over the completed
+Ridge-HPO subset, with median ratio 0.956 on $N=34$, 27/34 wins and
+$p=0.011$.
+
+The Ridge result supports the operator-kernel view.  In Ridge, preprocessing
+changes the regularized sample geometry.  Blender is the best main selector
+because it combines strong operator geometries through internal validation
+while preserving a single deployable Ridge model.
 
