 \section{Conclusion}
 
-Operator-adaptive calibration reframes NIRS preprocessing selection as a
-model-internal calibration problem.  AOM-PLS embeds linear operators through
-cross-covariance identities, AOM-Ridge embeds them through operator-induced
-kernels, and FastAOM extends the idea to screened chain and sparse
-multi-kernel exploration.  Across the available heterogeneous benchmark, the
-approach improves over standard linear references, remains competitive with
-much more expensive preprocessing-HPO baselines on their completed overlaps,
-and preserves auditable linear calibration objects.  The result is not a
-single universal preprocessing recipe, but a practical optimization layer for
-NIRS method development.
+Preprocessing screening for linear NIRS calibration works, but it is often
+implemented as an expensive outer loop.  For strict linear operators, PLS and
+Ridge provide identities that move the operator choice inside the calibration
+itself.  On the available AOM benchmark, the selected AOM-PLS and AOM-Ridge
+variants are comparable to or better than full preprocessing-HPO on completed
+overlaps, with a large time advantage for PLS.  The useful scientific message
+is therefore modest and practical: keep fitted corrections fold-local, fold
+strict linear operators into the model, and report every comparison on its
+paired denominator.
 
