+PLS remains the reference linear method for many NIRS calibration problems
+\citep{geladi1986partial,wold2001pls}.  NIPALS and SIMPLS offer two useful
+views: one iterative and score-based, the other covariance-based
+\citep{dejong1993simpls}.  Ridge regression provides a complementary
+regularized linear model \citep{hoerl1970ridge}; in dual form it depends on
+the sample Gram matrix, which makes preprocessing a change of kernel geometry
+\citep{scholkopf2002learning,hastie2009elements}.
 
-Ridge regression \citep{hoerl1970ridge} and kernel methods
-\citep{scholkopf2002learning,hastie2009elements} provide the second foundation
-for this work.  In the dual, Ridge depends on the sample Gram matrix, so
-preprocessing can be expressed as an operator-induced geometry.  FastAOM uses
-this view together with low-rank approximations and screened operator chains:
-operator dictionaries become a structured search space, not a collection of
-black-box pipelines.
+This work combines these two lines.  It keeps the chemometric interpretability
+of linear calibration, but treats strict preprocessing alternatives as
+operators that can be selected inside the model rather than as independent
+outer-loop pipelines.
 
